            "Every rhythm is read the same way, in the same order, so that nothing is missed and the vocabulary is shared. <strong>Step 1 — is there electrical activity, and is there a pulse?</strong> No activity is asystole (check the leads, the gain and a second lead before accepting it). <strong>Step 2 — the ventricular rate.</strong> At 25 mm/s each large square is 0.2 seconds and each small square 0.04 seconds; the rate is <strong>300 divided by the number of large squares between two R waves</strong> for a regular rhythm (one large square is 300, two is 150, three is 100, four is 75, five is 60, six is 50), or the number of QRS complexes in a six-second strip (30 large squares) multiplied by ten for an irregular one. Under 60 is bradycardia, over 100 is tachycardia. <strong>Step 3 — regularity.</strong> March a piece of paper along the R waves: regular, regularly irregular (a repeating pattern, as in second-degree block or ectopics every third beat) or irregularly irregular (no pattern — atrial fibrillation until proved otherwise). <strong>Step 4 — P waves.</strong> Are there P waves, are they upright in lead II, do they all look the same, and is there one before every QRS? Absent P waves with a chaotic baseline is atrial fibrillation; saw-tooth waves are flutter; more P waves than QRS complexes is a block. <strong>Step 5 — the PR interval</strong>, from the start of the P to the start of the QRS, normally <strong>0.12–0.20 seconds (3–5 small squares)</strong>: prolonged and constant is first-degree block, progressively lengthening until a beat drops is Wenckebach, variable with no relationship is complete heart block, short with a slurred upstroke is pre-excitation. <strong>Step 6 — the QRS width</strong>, normally <strong>under 0.12 seconds (3 small squares)</strong>: narrow means the impulse came from above the ventricles and travelled down the normal conducting system; broad means it came from the ventricles or travelled abnormally (bundle branch block, hyperkalaemia, tricyclic poisoning, a paced rhythm). Then look at the <strong>ST segment and T waves</strong> for ischaemia and electrolytes and the <strong>QT interval</strong> (corrected, under about 0.44–0.46 seconds) for the drugs and electrolytes that cause torsades. Describe the rhythm in those words — 'a regular broad-complex tachycardia at 180 with no visible P waves' — and the treatment follows from the description even when the name is uncertain.",
