            "Acute myocardial infarction is increasingly common in Ghana as hypertension, diabetes, smoking and obesity rise, and it is still missed — in women, in diabetics, in the young, and in the patient who calls it 'indigestion'. The classical presentation is <strong>central crushing or heavy chest pain lasting more than 20 minutes, radiating to the left arm, jaw or neck, with sweating, nausea, breathlessness and a sense of doom</strong>, not relieved by rest or nitrates; the <strong>atypical presentations</strong> — epigastric pain and vomiting, breathlessness alone, collapse, new confusion in the elderly, 'heartburn' that radiates to the jaw, or a diabetic with no pain at all (the 'silent' infarct) — are just as lethal and are suspected in any patient over 40 with risk factors who feels suddenly unwell, sweaty and sick. The first ten minutes are the nurse's: a <strong>12-lead ECG within ten minutes of arrival</strong>, read by someone competent and repeated every 15–30 minutes if the pain persists or changes and at six hours; <strong>aspirin 300 mg chewed</strong> (unless truly allergic — a stomach upset is not an allergy); <strong>glyceryl trinitrate</strong> sublingually, repeated at five-minute intervals up to three doses <strong>if the systolic pressure is above 90</strong> and the patient has not taken sildenafil in the last 24 hours; <strong>oxygen only if the saturation is below 94%</strong> (routine oxygen in a well-saturated patient increases infarct size); intravenous access with blood for troponin, electrolytes, glucose and a full count; <strong>morphine 2.5–5 mg IV with an antiemetic</strong> titrated to pain; continuous ECG monitoring with the <strong>defibrillator beside the bed</strong>, because the first hours after infarction are when ventricular fibrillation strikes; and bed rest with the patient told not to walk to the toilet.",
            "The ECG decides the pathway. <strong>ST elevation</strong> — 1 mm or more in two adjacent limb leads or 2 mm in adjacent chest leads, or a new left bundle branch block, with reciprocal depression in the opposite leads — is a <strong>STEMI</strong>: a coronary artery is completely blocked and the muscle is dying by the minute, and the patient needs <strong>reperfusion</strong> — primary angioplasty within 120 minutes where a catheter laboratory exists (Accra and Kumasi), or <strong>thrombolysis with streptokinase or alteplase within 12 hours of pain onset</strong>, ideally within 30 minutes of arrival, everywhere else, after checking the contraindications (recent stroke or surgery, active bleeding, severe uncontrolled hypertension, a previous streptokinase dose) and with the nurse watching for the bleeding, hypotension and reperfusion arrhythmias that follow. The territory is read from the leads: <strong>II, III and aVF are inferior</strong> (right coronary — watch for bradycardia, heart block and right ventricular infarction, in which nitrates drop the pressure dangerously), <strong>V1–V4 are anterior/septal</strong> (left anterior descending — the biggest infarcts, with heart failure and cardiogenic shock), <strong>I, aVL, V5–V6 are lateral</strong> (circumflex), and tall R waves with ST depression in V1–V3 suggest a posterior infarct. <strong>ST depression and T-wave inversion</strong> with a positive troponin is an <strong>NSTEMI</strong>; a normal or non-specific ECG with a rising troponin is also an infarct; and a normal ECG with a normal troponin at six hours and again later makes infarction unlikely but does not exclude unstable angina. All receive antiplatelet therapy (aspirin plus clopidogrel), anticoagulation with heparin, a beta-blocker and a statin unless contraindicated, treatment of pain, heart failure and arrhythmias, and cardiology review; and the nurse watches for the complications of the first days — arrhythmias, heart failure and pulmonary oedema, cardiogenic shock, a new murmur from a ruptured papillary muscle or septum, pericarditis, and the re-infarction announced by returning pain. Unstable angina — rest pain, new pain, crescendo pain with a normal troponin — is admitted and treated as an acute coronary syndrome, not sent home with antacids.",
