            "<strong>Hyperkalaemia</strong> — in renal failure, after a crush injury or burns, with potassium-sparing diuretics and ACE inhibitors, in diabetic ketoacidosis, after massive transfusion of old blood, in adrenal crisis or from a potassium infusion run too fast — shows a predictable sequence on the ECG that <strong>appears earliest as tall, narrow, peaked ('tented') T waves</strong>, then flattening and loss of the P wave, a lengthening PR interval, <strong>widening of the QRS</strong>, and finally a slurred <strong>sine wave</strong>, ventricular fibrillation or asystole; the correlation with the level is loose, and a potassium of 7 mmol/L with a normal ECG is still an emergency. The laboratory telephones a potassium of 7.1: the nurse <strong>repeats the sample if haemolysis is suspected without delaying treatment, puts the patient on a monitor, stops all potassium (the infusion, the supplement, the ACE inhibitor, the Ringer's), tells the doctor immediately</strong> and prepares the drugs in order: <strong>calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL (or calcium chloride 10 mL) IV over 5–10 minutes first, to stabilise the myocardium</strong> — it does nothing to the potassium but it protects the heart within minutes and is repeated if the ECG changes persist; then <strong>soluble insulin 10 units in 25 g of glucose (50 mL of 50% or 250 mL of 10%) over 15–30 minutes</strong>, which drives potassium into the cells for a few hours, with the glucose monitored for the hypoglycaemia that follows; <strong>nebulised salbutamol 10–20 mg</strong>, which does the same; sodium bicarbonate if acidotic; and then the measures that actually remove potassium — a loop diuretic if passing urine, an exchange resin, and <strong>dialysis</strong> for the anuric patient. Insulin and dextrose without the calcium first, in a patient with ECG changes, risks arrest in the twenty minutes before the potassium moves.",
